  <body>
    <div class="proto-badge" aria-hidden="true">Design exploration — Shifting Environments / B1: Adventurous palette</div>

    <div class="env-skill">
      <header class="topline">
        <a class="wordmark" href="/design-explore/environments-adventurous-palette/">
          <span class="wordmark-mark">sw</span>spellingwords
        </a>
        <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Prototype navigation">
          <a href="/design-explore/environments-adventurous-palette/">Home</a>
        </nav>
      </header>

      <div class="page-fade" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <main>
        <p class="crumb">
          <a href="/design-explore/environments-adventurous-palette/">Home</a> /
          <a href="/design-explore/environments-adventurous-palette/">Skills</a> / SH Digraph Words
        </p>

        <header class="hero">
          <h1>SH Digraph Words</h1>
          <p class="hero-desc">
            Two letters, one sound. The <strong>sh</strong> digraph is one of the first consonant
            teams kids learn to hear and write as a single unit, and it shows up everywhere from
            <em>ship</em> to <em>fish</em>.
          </p>
        </header>

        <section class="answer-block" aria-labelledby="what-h">
          <h2 id="what-h">What are SH digraph words?</h2>
          <p>
            SH digraph words contain the letter pair <strong>sh</strong>, which together make one
            sound &mdash; the quiet &ldquo;hush&rdquo; sound, as in <em>shop</em> or <em>wish</em>.
            Neither letter keeps its own sound; <strong>s</strong> and <strong>h</strong> combine
            into something new, which is exactly what makes a digraph a digraph rather than a
            blend.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="list-h" class="word-list-section">
          <h2 id="list-h">Word list</h2>
          <ul class="word-list">
            <li><span class="word">ship</span></li>
            <li><span class="word">shop</span></li>
            <li><span class="word">shut</span></li>
            <li><span class="word">wish</span></li>
            <li><span class="word">brush</span></li>
            <li><span class="word">dish</span></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="understanding-h">
          <h2 id="understanding-h">Understanding the pattern</h2>
          <p>
            The <strong>sh</strong> digraph can appear at the start of a word, as in <em>ship</em>
            and <em>shop</em>, or at the end, as in <em>wish</em> and <em>dish</em>. Either way, it
            behaves as a single spelling unit &mdash; kids should be encouraged to write it as one
            chunk, not sound out an /s/ and a /h/ separately and try to glue them together.
          </p>
          <p>
            This matters for spelling in particular because the <strong>sh</strong> sound has only
            one common spelling in early reading, unlike trickier sounds such as long <em>a</em>,
            which can be spelled several different ways. That makes SH words a reliable, confidence
            -building unit: once a child hears the sound, there&rsquo;s really only one letter pair
            to reach for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where it gets more interesting is in longer words and word endings &mdash; words like
            <em>brush</em> and <em>crash</em> pair the digraph with a consonant blend, which is
            worth calling out explicitly once the short, simple SH words feel comfortable.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="compare-h" class="compare-section">
          <h2 id="compare-h">SH vs. CH</h2>
          <p class="compare-intro">
            SH and CH are easy to mix up early on, since both are two-letter consonant teams that
            make a single new sound. Saying the word slowly usually settles it.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Word</th>
                  <th scope="col">Digraph</th>
                  <th scope="col">Sound</th>
                  <th scope="col">Example sentence</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>ship</td>
                  <td>sh</td>
                  <td>&ldquo;hush&rdquo; sound</td>
                  <td>The ship left the harbor at dawn.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>chip</td>
                  <td>ch</td>
                  <td>&ldquo;ch&rdquo; sound</td>
                  <td>One chip fell off the plate.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>wish</td>
                  <td>sh</td>
                  <td>&ldquo;hush&rdquo; sound</td>
                  <td>She made a wish before blowing out the candle.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>witch</td>
                  <td>ch</td>
                  <td>&ldquo;ch&rdquo; sound</td>
                  <td>The witch wore a tall hat.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
          <h2 id="faq-h">Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does every SH sound get spelled with the letters S and H?</h3>
            <p>
              In early reading, yes &mdash; almost always. Later on, kids will meet other spellings
              for the same sound (like the &ldquo;ti&rdquo; in <em>nation</em> or the &ldquo;ci&rdquo;
              in <em>special</em>), but those come well after the SH digraph is solid, so it&rsquo;s
              safe to treat <strong>sh</strong> as the default spelling for now.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Is SH a blend or a digraph?</h3>
            <p>
              A digraph. In a blend, like <em>bl</em> in <em>black</em>, you can still hear both
              letters. In a digraph, like <em>sh</em>, the two letters merge into one new sound
              with no trace of the individual <em>s</em> or <em>h</em>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="cta-block">
          <div class="cta-inner">
            <div>
              <p class="cta-eyebrow">Ready when you are</p>
              <h2>Practice these words</h2>
              <p>Six words, read aloud one at a time, typed by hand.</p>
            </div>
            <a class="cta-btn" href="/design-explore/environments-adventurous-palette/skill">Start Practicing &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="related" aria-labelledby="related-h">
